## Ferroelectricity, SSFLC, bistability and all that
**Ingolf Dahl**, MINA, Chalmers University, S-412 96 Göteborg, Sweden.

A critical review of S. T. Lagerwall's "Ferroelectric and Antiferroelectric
Liquid Crystals". Central physics content: the operational distinction between
a genuine bistable (double-well) ferroelectric and a nonlinear lossy
(monostable) material that only mimics a hysteresis loop.

### Central testable statement
"If we have two bistable states in the material, with a potential barrier
between, the width of the hysteresis loop should be independent of the
frequency, while a nonlinear, lossy material should have a hysteresis loop
width that is approx. proportional to the frequency."

Switching-time relation cited from Lagerwall (schematic):
$$ \tau \sim \frac{\gamma}{P\,E} $$
where $\tau$ = switching time, $\gamma$ = viscosity, $P$ = polarization,
$E$ = electric field.

